3) Touched path (direct + Quick Adjust) — CONFIRMED (oracle: healthcare
   slider→1800 saves 1800, portfolio slider→2,500,000 saves it, Quick
   Adjust healthcare mirror→1900 saves 1900, all status 200).

4) Display vs aria-valuetext ("matches") — investigated hard, found a real
   but NON-SURVIVING defect. Pure server-rendered HTML uses TWO different
   round-half rules for the same figure: the visible span's `formatNumber`
   (Go `%.0f`, round-half-to-even) vs the new `aria-valuetext`'s
   `formatDollars`/`formatWholeDollars` (`int64(v+0.5)`, round-half-up).
   Verified via Go template render at $7,386.50 and $1,800.50 (portfolio
   and healthcare-cost sliders): span shows "$7,386"/"$1,800",
   aria-valuetext shows "$7,387"/"$1,801" — a real divergence, and it
   repeats on every currency slider this task touched (same pairing
   pattern). BUT: re-verified against the ACTUAL rendered DOM in a real
   browser after JS settles (not just the Go HTML) — `DOMContentLoaded` →
   `syncAllQuickAdjustControls()` → `syncQuickAdjustKey()` immediately
   re-writes BOTH the display span and aria-valuetext from the SAME JS
   function (`computeQuickAdjustDisplayText`, single Math.round-based
   formatter), for every canonical control, unconditionally, on every page
   load and after every htmx swap (`htmx:afterSwap`/`oobAfterSwap`). Probed
   live: portfolio $7,386.50 → both display AND aria-valuetext read
   "$7,387" post-settle; healthcare $1,800.50 → both "$1,801"; ACA-after-
   employer $933.50 → both "$934". The FINAL observable state a user or
   screen-reader encounters is consistent — the Go-side formatter mismatch
   is a sub-100ms pre-hydration artifact this JS-heavy htmx app always
   overwrites before any realistic interaction. Per the checker
   constitution's rule 6 (verify the counterexample against real behavior,
   not a plausible reading), this does not stand as a criterion-4 FAIL.
   Reporting as an OBSERVATION for the backlog (defensive-coding gap: a
   no-JS/JS-error path would show the Go-side $1 mismatch permanently; the
   two Go formatters should be reconciled or one should defer to the other
   for consistency's own sake).

5) ids/behavior kept — spot-checked; all existing ids preserved
   (`healthcare-monthly-cost-{{.ID}}`, `portfolio-slider`, etc., new `-exact`
   ids added alongside, none renamed). Found one SPLIT-SOURCE-shaped but
   NOT-actually-broken interaction: `whatif-rate-assumptions.js:146,213`
   query `input[name="portfolio_value"]` / `input[name="investment_return"]`
   — these selectors now resolve to the new HIDDEN canonical field instead
   of the visible range (since the name moved there). Traced + live-tested
   (dragged #portfolio-slider, watched #td-amount-display/#roth-amount-
   display/#taxable-amount-display recompute correctly from the freshly-
   synced hidden value): correct, because the document-level capturing
   'input' listener (whatif-quick-adjust.js:262, registered with
   `useCapture=true`) that proxies a mirror's dragged value into the hidden
   canonical field always fires BEFORE the target element's own inline
   `oninput="updateAccountAmounts()"` handler. Functionally correct today,
   but fragile (depends on listener registration order, untested) —
   OBSERVATION for backlog, not a FAIL.

